==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|ducket}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dewk-}}
From {{bor|en|frm|ducat}}, late {{der|en|fro|ducat}}, from {{der|en|roa-oit|it|ducato}}, from {{der|en|ML.|ducatus}}, from oblique stem of {{m|la|dux||duke; leader}}. {{doublet|en|duchy}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdʌkət/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-ducat.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌkət|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|historical}} A gold coin minted by various European nations.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|2|8|passage=Shylock: "My daughter! O my '''ducats'''! Fled with a Christian! O my Christian '''ducats'''! Justice! the law! my '''ducats''', and my daughter!..."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=166|passage=But when the innkeeper saw what kind of goat the lad had, he thought this was a goat worth having, so when the lad had fallen asleep, he took another goat which couldn't make any golden '''ducats''', and put that in its place.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[coin]] of the major denomination ([[dollar]], [[euro]], etc.); money in general.
# {{lb|en|US|theater|transport|slang}} A [[ticket]].
#* {{quote-book|en|1972|origyear=1931|{{w|Dashiell Hammett}}|{{w|The Glass Key}}|publisher=Vintage Books|chapter=IV. The Dog House|page=73|passage=Ned Beaumont said, "Well, I've got a '''ducat''' that reads to there, anyway.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1946|author=[[w:George Johnston (novelist)|George Johnston]]|title=Skyscrapers in the Mist|page=88|text=Customers are usually enticed in with a "'''ducat'''", or pass for one free dance.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=June 11 1949|journal=Billboard
|passage={{...}} the lines at the box office since '''ducats''' went on sale publicly Wednesday {{...}}}}

